+
+Limiting concurrency
+--------------------
+
+By default, all file I/O operations use the default capacity limiter. If you wish to
+more explicitly control the concurrency, you can pass a custom :class:`CapacityLimiter`
+to :func:`open_file`, :func:`wrap_file` or :class:`Path` (or its various class
+methods)::
+
+    from anyio import CapacityLimiter, open_file, run
+
+
+    async def main():
+        limiter = CapacityLimiter(5)
+        async with open_file('/foo/bar', limiter=limiter) as fp:
+            contents = await fp.read()
+
+    run(main)
+
+
+Immutable limiter reconfiguration
+--------------------------------
+
+``path.with_limiter(limiter)`` returns a distinct path at the same filesystem
+location. Pass ``None`` to select the default thread limiter. The source path,
+existing derived paths and already opened files keep their previous limiter.
+The limiter object itself remains shared, so updating ``total_tokens`` affects
+every object using it.
+
+``Path(existing_path)`` and ``Path(existing_path, "child")`` inherit the first
+argument's limiter when no limiter keyword is supplied. Explicit ``limiter=None``
+clears it. A later Path argument never overrides the first argument's choice.
